# Migrating `TimedExecutor` to .NET 8

## Key Problem

`Thread.Abort()` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` throw `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8. The cooperative cancellation pattern using `CancellationToken` is the replacement.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Block caller like the original thread.Join(timeout) did
            task.Wait(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }

        // Unwrap AggregateException for parity with original
        if (task.IsFaulted)
            throw task.Exception!;

        return task.Result;
    }

    // Async overload (preferred in modern code)
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await work(cts.Token).WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

## Usage

```csharp
var executor = new TimedExecutor();

// Sync
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(ct =>
{
    while (!ct.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        // do work in chunks, check token periodically
    }
    ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    return 42;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

// Async
var asyncResult = await executor.ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync(async ct =>
{
    await httpClient.GetStringAsync(url, ct);
    return "done";
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10));
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **No forced termination** | Unlike `Thread.Abort()`, cancellation is *cooperative*. If `work` ignores the token, it keeps running. There is no safe replacement for hard-kill semantics in-process. |
| **`default(T)` race condition** | The original code had a race: `result` could be read before the thread wrote it. `Task.Run` + `Task.Result` eliminates this. |
| **Leaked threads** | Original code aborted leaked threads. With cooperative cancellation, a runaway task leaks a thread-pool thread. For untrusted code, run it out-of-process. |
| **`ThreadAbortException` gone** | Any catch blocks for `ThreadAbortException` in called code become dead code — remove them. |
| **`Task.Wait` vs `await`** | `Task.Wait()` blocks the thread and can deadlock in UI/ASP.NET sync contexts. Prefer the async overload. |

## If You Truly Need Hard Cancellation

For uncooperative code (e.g., third-party library with no cancellation support):

```csharp
// Run in a separate process and kill the process on timeout.
// This is the only safe equivalent to Thread.Abort().
var process = Process.Start("worker.exe", args);
if (!process.WaitForExit(timeout))
{
    process.Kill(entireProcessTree: true);
    throw new TimeoutException("...");
}
```

There is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort()` in .NET 8 — this is by design, as thread aborts could corrupt shared state.
